    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this certificate fragment.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 (page 1-4 of 5), issued by NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1), for ABB Limited, water meter type AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R and FET4*.\n\nKey details:\n\n**certificate:**\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\" (This OIML Certificate is issued under scheme A)\n- project_number: \"2254799\"\n- page_total: 5 (Page 1 of 5)\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2020-08-14\" (14 August 2020)\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\n**issuing_authority:**\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M. Boudewijns\"\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" (NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- person_title: not explicitly given. \"Certification Board\" appears near the signature... Actually \"Person responsible: M. Boudewijns\" — no title given. Omit.\n\n**applicants[]:**\n- name: \"ABB Limited\"\n- address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n\n**manufacturers[]:** same as applicant (\"Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited\")\n\n**certified_type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\" — the category list mentions \"Water meter\"? The schema lists examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\", ... Water meter fits.\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"] — the type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R and FET4 *\". Hmm, the * indicates XX placeholder: first X is 1 or 3, second X is 1, 2, 8, or 9. So type designations could be recorded as [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"].\n- module_designation: null — not applicable/mentioned. Actually there are components: flow transmitter and flow sensor. But module_designation field — leave null or omit. I'll omit.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\"\n\n**model_family:**\nThe family of instruments covers meter sizes DN40 through DN350. Model IDs could be DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350.\n\nFamily name: \"AquaMaster4\"? Not explicit as \"family\". The doc says \"Table 2 gives an overview of the general characteristics of the family of instruments.\" Family name could be \"AquaMaster4\". I'll set family_name to \"AquaMaster4\" — that's the type name which is the family. Actually rule: \"explicit family name if any, else null\". The type AquaMaster4 with variants FEW4XX.R and FET4*. Hmm. I could set family_name: \"AquaMaster4\".\n\nModels: meter sizes DN40...DN350. Also note there are two configurations: AC mains powered (Table 2) vs Battery/Renewable energy powered (Table 3). And accuracy class variants 1, 2, 1&2.\n\nI think the cleanest approach: models are meter sizes (DN40–DN350), and config_level entries handle power supply axis (AC mains vs battery/renewable), with attribute values per accuracy class? Hmm, accuracy class is another dimension.\n\nActually the tables are attribute × (size, accuracy class) matrices. The characteristics per row: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio Q3/Q1, accuracy class.\n\nFor model_level, I could create model_ids like \"DN40\", \"DN50\", etc. and attributes q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. But the values vary by accuracy class and power supply. This becomes a config-level multi-dimensional thing.\n\nOptions:\n1. Use matrix_tables for Table 2 and Table 3 (preserve as-is), and populate model_family.models with the sizes.\n2. Add model_level entries where values are unambiguous (e.g., Q3 is the same across accuracy classes for a given size: DN40 Q3=25 for both class 1 and 2 in both power configs; DN50 Q3=40; etc.).\n\nLet me check Q3 by size:\nTable 2 (AC mains):\n- DN40: 25 (both classes)\n- DN50: 40 (both)\n- DN65: 63 (both)\n- DN80: 100 (both)\n- DN100: 160 (both)\n- DN125: 160 (both)\n- DN150: 400\n- DN200: 630\n- DN250: 1000\n- DN300: 1600\n- DN350: 1600\n\nTable 3 (battery/renewable):\n- DN40: 25\n- DN50: 40\n- DN65: 63 (both)\n- DN80: 100\n- DN100: 160\n- DN125: 160\n- DN150: 400\n- DN200: 630\n- DN250: 1000\n- DN300: 1600\n- DN350: 1600\n\nSo Q3 per size is the same regardless of power supply and accuracy class. Good — q3 can be model_level.\n\nQ4 = 1.25 × Q3, same per size. q4 model_level works.\n\nQ1 and Q2 vary by accuracy class AND power supply. Those should be config_level with axis like \"meter_size × accuracy_class × power_supply\"? Or captured only in matrix_tables. The schema says config_level for attribute × config-axis matrix. I can do:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", axis: \"meter_size (rows) × accuracy_class × power_supply_config\", values: [{condition: \"DN40 / class 1 / AC mains\", value: 0.05, unit_symbol: \"m³/h\"}, ...]\n\nThat would be a lot of entries but doable. Alternatively, rely on matrix_tables to preserve the full data and just add model_level for the size-invariant attributes (q3, q4) and maybe config_level for q1/q2/ratio.\n\nLet me think about what's cleanest and most faithful:\n\n- matrix_tables: two entries, \"Table 2 General characteristics of the family of instruments – Reduce bore – AC mains powered\" and \"Table 3 ... Battery / Renewable energy powered\". Columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. Rows as objects with numbers (convert commas to dots).\n\n- model_family.models: [{\"model_id\": \"DN40\"}, ..., {\"model_id\": \"DN350\"}]\n\n- characteristics.model_level: q3, q4 per model (size-invariant). Maybe also accuracy_classes... no, accuracy class is part of type-level: \"Accuracy class: 1 and 2\".\n\n- characteristics.type_level: measuring principle, environmental class (varies by component — flow transmitter B, flow sensor O — this is component-related; could be type_level as string or component-level), electromagnetic environment E1, water temperature class T50 (+0.1/+50 °C), MAP 1.6 MPa (16 bar), orientation, flow profile sensitivity class U0/D0, reverse flow, pressure loss class (varies by size: Δp40 for DN40/DN50, Δp65 for larger — that's model-dependent! Actually it varies by size, so config_level or model_level), power supply (multiple), software identification.\n\nLet me structure:\n\ntype_level:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm, or recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"1\", \"2\"]. Both. The characteristic row exists too. I'll include accuracy_classes in recommendation, and maybe skip duplicating in type_level. Actually the table lists it; I can include it. It's fine to have accuracy_class in type_level as string \"1 and 2\"? The schema says value can be string. I'll include it.\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E1\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\", ...} with range? The value is \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". I'd do value: \"T50\" plus maybe water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}. The +0.1°C is minimum... T50 class means water temp from 0.1°C to 50°C. So:\n  - water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n  - water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} plus note 16 bar? Could add footnote or separate. I'll do value 1.6 MPa, and maybe a second entry maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? Better: keep one entry value: 1.6, unit MPa. The \"(16 bar)\" is redundant conversion. Could include footnote. I'll keep simple.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"} — lowercase fine? Keep as given: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\". Strings keep original text mostly.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"}\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- software_identification_version: {value: \"03.00.03\"}\n- software_checksum: {value: \"B26AFE6B\"}\n\nEnvironmental class varies by component: flow transmitter B, flow sensor O. Could be config_level with axis \"component\": [{condition: \"flow transmitter\", value: \"B\"}, {condition: \"flow sensor\", value: \"O\"}]. Or components[] entries. The characteristics table mention: \"Environmental class: B (installed in buildings) for flow transmitter; O (installed outdoors) for flow sensor\". Also temperature range ambient varies: +5/+55 for flow transmitter, -25/+55 for flow sensor.\n\nI could use config_level:\n- attribute: \"environmental_class\", axis: \"component\", values: [{condition: \"flow transmitter\", value: \"B\"}, {condition: \"flow sensor\", value: \"O\"}]\n- attribute: \"ambient_temperature_range\", axis: \"component\", values: [{condition: \"flow transmitter\", value: {min: 5, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}, {condition: \"flow sensor\", value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}]\n\nThe values follow same structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Good.\n\nPressure loss class varies by size:\n- attribute: \"pressure_loss_class\", axis: \"meter_size\", values: [{condition: \"DN40\", value: \"Δp 40\"}, {condition: \"DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\"}, {condition: \"larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 65\"}]. With pressure loss value 0.40 bar / 0.65 bar. Could do:\n  - {condition: \"DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}\n  - {condition: \"> DN50\", value: \"Δp 65 (0.65 bar)\"}\n  Or numeric: value 0.4, unit_symbol \"bar\", with class in condition? Hmm. I'll do value: \"Δp 40\" / \"Δp 65\" and separate pressure_loss values... Simplest faithful: \n  values: [{condition: \"DN40 and DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}, {condition: \"larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 65 (0.65 bar)\"}]\n\nPower supply: three options. These are type-level characteristics with variants:\n- power_supply_ac_mains_voltage: {value: {min: 85, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- power_supply_dc_voltage: {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} (renewable energy / DC powered)\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"} and battery_operating_range: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\nRule 3 example: `110-240V AC 50/60Hz` split into three characteristics. I'll follow that pattern.\n\nmodel_level: \n- permanent_flow_rate_q3 per model (size): DN40→25 ... etc.\n- overload_flow_rate_q4 per model.\nAlso could add q1, q2 but those vary by class & power — put in config_level or leave to matrix_tables. I think matrix_tables preserve them fully; adding config_level duplicates. But the schema emphasizes model_level extraction. Let me add q1 and q2 as config_level with axis \"accuracy_class and power supply\" — condition strings like \"DN40, class 1, AC mains\". That's 38 rows × 2 attributes = lots of entries. Hmm, Table 2 has 19 rows, Table 3 has 18 rows. That's 37 conditions each for q1 and q2, plus ratio_q3_q1 (37). That's heavy but complete.\n\nAlternative: rely on matrix_tables for the full multi-dimensional data (schema says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\") and provide model_level for size-invariant attributes. I think a balanced approach:\n\n- model_level: q3, q4 (invariant per size).\n- config_level: q1, q2, ratio_q3_q1 with axis \"meter size × accuracy class × power supply\" — condition strings combining. Actually, that duplicates matrix tables entirely. The schema explicitly says matrix_tables is for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". q1/q2/ratio with 3 dimensions (size, class, power) don't fit model_level (single axis). I could still do config_level for q1/q2... \n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe models should be size × power config? E.g., model_id \"DN40 (AC mains)\" vs \"DN40 (battery)\". Then model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio per model × class... still class dimension remains.\n\nI think cleanest: models = 11 meter sizes. matrix_tables = two full tables. model_level = q3, q4. config_level = accuracy_class per... no, accuracy class: each size available in class 1 and 2 (DN65+ in table 2 as \"1 & 2\" combined, meaning both). So accuracy_classes [1,2] at type level. Fine.\n\nMaybe add config_level for q1/q2/ratio? I'll skip to avoid enormous duplication — the matrix_tables preserve it. Actually wait — the instructions say \"Matrix detection — MOST IMPORTANT... EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". In the R137 example they populated model_level for qmax, qt, qmin. By analogy, I should populate model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4. But q1/q2 vary by accuracy class within a size. Options: model_id could be \"DN40 class 1\" etc.? That inflates models to ~37. Hmm.\n\nActually, look at Table 2: DN65 \"1 & 2\" single row: Q1=0.063. While DN40 has separate rows for class 1 (Q1=0.05) and class 2 (Q1=0.025). So class matters for some sizes.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll do:\n- models: 11 sizes.\n- model_level: q3 (permanent_flow_rate_q3), q4 (overload_flow_rate_q4) — invariant.\n- config_level: minimum_flow_rate_q1 and transitional_flow_rate_q2 with axis \"meter size × accuracy class × power supply\", values as conditions like \"DN40 / class 1 / AC mains powered\". Also ratio_q3_q1.\n\nHmm, that's ~111 value objects. That's a lot of JSON but accurate. Alternatively, since matrix_tables fully capture these, maybe config_level is redundant. The schema: \"config_level[]: attribute × config-axis matrix\". The two tables differ by power supply axis. Within a table, rows are size × class.\n\nI'll compromise: include config_level for q1, q2, ratio_q3_q1 — complete extraction. Actually, let me count: Table 2 rows: DN40×2, DN50×2, DN65×1, DN80×1, DN100×2, DN125×2, DN150×1, DN200×1, DN250×2, DN300×2, DN350×2 = 18 rows. Let me recount: DN40: 2, DN50: 2, DN65: 1, DN80: 1, DN100: 2, DN125: 2, DN150: 1, DN200: 1, DN250: 2, DN300: 2, DN350: 2 → 2+2+1+1+2+2+1+1+2+2+2 = 18.\n\nTable 3 rows: DN40:1, DN50:1, DN65:2, DN80:2, DN100:2, DN125:2, DN150:1, DN200:1, DN250:2, DN300:2, DN350:2 = 18.\n\nTotal 36 config rows × 3 attributes (q1, q2, ratio) = 108 values, plus q3/q4 model_level 22 values. That's big but let me consider whether to include ratio in config_level — ratio_q3_q1: DN40 AC: class1 500, class2 1000; battery: 400. Varies. Yes include.\n\nHmm, actually, I wonder if that's over-engineering. The matrix_tables rows will contain all these values anyway. The purpose of config_level is when attributes vary by config axis \"not by model\". Here the two tables ARE the power-supply axis: Table 2 = AC mains, Table 3 = battery/renewable. Within each table, q1/q2/ratio vary by size AND class.\n\nGiven \"Matrix detection — MOST IMPORTANT\", I'll be thorough. I'll include config_level entries for q1, q2, and ratio_q3_q1 with axis \"meter_size × accuracy_class × power_supply\" and condition strings. And model_level for q3, q4.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider using model_id = \"DN40\" and putting q1/q2 in model_level won't work due to class variance. Alternatively define models as size+class? Like \"DN40 class 1\"? The certificate itself treats meter size as the model (family of instruments by size). I'll keep sizes as models.\n\nLet me now build the matrix_tables.\n\nTable 2 (Reduce bore – AC mains powered):\ncolumns: [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\nrows (converting commas):\n- DN40, class 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n- DN40, class 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50, class 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, class 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, class 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, class 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, class 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, class 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, class 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, class 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, class 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, class 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, class 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, class 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n\nTable 3 (Reduce bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered):\n- DN40, 1&2: 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n- DN50, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n- DN65, class 1: 0.39375, 0.63, 63, 78.75, 160\n- DN65, class 2: 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n- DN80, class 1: 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n- DN80, class 2: 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n- DN100, class 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN100, class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN125, class 1: 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n- DN125, class 2: 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n- DN150, 1&2: 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n- DN200, 1&2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n- DN250, class 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\n- DN250, class 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n- DN300, class 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN300, class 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n- DN350, class 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\n- DN350, class 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n\nFootnotes for both tables: the \"Please note\" conditions. I'll add them to matrix_tables.footnotes or as a description. The note applies to both tables. I'll add footnotes array per table with the four bullet conditions, or description summarizing. I'll put them in footnotes:\n- \"Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013(E)\"\n- \"Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6\"\n- \"Values mentioned for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and the ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25\"\n- \"The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40\"\n\nNote: ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6 → 1.6 (convert decimal). Q4/Q3 = 1.25.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\" (canonical — \"R 49-1 (2013)\" → R49)\n- edition: 2013? The schema says edition (int). \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — the edition year is 2013. R49 has parts 1, 2, 3. Should I record part? Schema doesn't have a part field. I could put id \"R49-1\"? Rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". For R49-1, canonical might be \"R49-1\" since the certificate conformance is with part 1. Hmm. I'll use \"R49-1\" as id and edition 2013. Actually, \"id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\")\" — the point is to strip \"OIML\" prefix. R49-1 is fine.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2] or [\"1\", \"2\"]? Schema says accuracy_classes[] — probably list of strings or ints. I'll use [\"1\", \"2\"]... Accuracy classes are typically strings. Hmm, the example doesn't specify. I'll use [\"1\", \"2\"] as strings to be safe. Actually numbers might be fine too. I'll go with strings \"1\" and \"2\".\n\ntest_reports:\n- id: \"NMi-2254799-01\", date: \"2020-08-14\", pages: 235, role: \"type_evaluation_report\" (it's the OIML Type Evaluation Report — the cert says \"represented by the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" and the report listed is the associated report). Role: type_evaluation_report.\n\nDocumentation folder no. T11851-1 — that's a documentation_file. test_reports[] role options include documentation_file. I'll add: id: \"T11851-1\", role: \"documentation_file\". No date/pages. Hmm, \"Documentation folder no. T11851-1\". Include it as {\"id\": \"T11851-1\", \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nrevision_history: not present. Omit.\n\nfootnotes: The \"*)\" note on page 1: \"The type designation XX can be on the first X for 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced) and the second X can be 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only.\" — this is a footnote with marker \"*\". I'll extract:\n- marker: \"*\", text: \"The type designation XX can be on the first X for 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced) and the second X can be 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only).\"\n\nThe note about flow rates — I put in matrix footnotes.\n\ncertificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"2254799\"\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2020-08-14\" (14 August 2020)\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M. Boudewijns\"\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]. The original: \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R and FET4 *\". Maybe record as [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]. Keep the * as footnote marker? The * refers to footnote. I'll write \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\" — hmm, trailing punctuation rule says no footnote markers in labels; for type designations it's part of the designation. I'll keep \"FET4*\" but could add footnote_markers? The schema for type_designations is just strings. I'll keep \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\".\n\n- module_designation: null or omit. Omit (don't fabricate). Actually could the flow sensor / flow transmitter be modules? There are components mentioned in characteristics (flow transmitter, flow sensor). components[]: include only if cert names sub-assemblies — it does: \"flow transmitter\" and \"flow sensor\". I'll add components:\n  - role: \"flow transmitter\", type_designations: [] (none given), characteristics... hmm, environmental class B, temp +5/+55. But I already capture those in config_level. Components entries with role and maybe characteristics: {environmental_class: \"B (installed in buildings)\", temperature_range_ambient: {...}}. Components schema: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I can put component-specific characteristics there.\n  - role: \"flow sensor\": environmental class O, temp -25/+55, reverse flow measurement.\n\nLet me do that instead of config_level for those two attributes — cleaner. And keep pressure_loss_class as config_level (axis: meter_size).\n\nHmm, but config_level with axis \"component\" is also legit. Either way. I'll use components[] with characteristics for transmitter/sensor-specific stuff. Actually the components schema characteristics{} shape isn't specified strictly — \"sub-characteristics\". I'll use structured values there too:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {role: \"Flow transmitter\", type_designations: [], characteristics: {environmental_class: {value: \"B (installed in buildings)\"}, temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: 5, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}}},\n  {role: \"Flow sensor\", type_designations: [], characteristics: {environmental_class: {value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}, temperature_range_ambient: {value: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}, reverse_flow: {value: \"intended to measure reverse flow\"}}}\n]\n\nHmm, but type_designations empty might violate... \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\" — it names roles but no type designations for them. I'll include with empty arrays. Or maybe better to capture environmental class and temperature in config_level with axis \"component\" and skip components[]. The components would be nice for the record. I'll do both? No, avoid duplication. \n\nDecision: use components[] with their characteristics (environmental class, ambient temp range), and also add config_level? No. I'll use components[]. And put pressure_loss_class in config_level (axis meter_size), power supplies in type_level, water temp in type_level, etc.\n\nActually wait — environmental class and ambient temp in type_level table row... The table gives both. If I use components, type_level won't have them. That's fine and arguably more correct.\n\nNow type_level characteristics:\n- measuring_principle: {value: \"Electromagnetic\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: \"1 and 2\"} — hmm, or omit since recommendation has accuracy_classes. I'll include it as it's in Table 1. Value: \"1 and 2\". Hmm, \"Lists of values... is a MODEL MATRIX\" — no, classes don't vary per model here; all sizes available in both classes. Type-level string is fine.\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E1\"}\n- water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"} and water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}. Original \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"} — plus \"16 bar\" parenthetical. I could add footnote_markers? Not a footnote. I'll add a second representation? Keep simple: value 1.6 MPa. Maybe add note in... skip.\n- orientation: {value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0\"} with detail? Original: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Value string keep whole: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Fine.\n- reverse_flow: {value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\"}\n- power_supply_ac_voltage: {value: {min: 85, max: 240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- power_supply_frequency: {value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}\n- power_supply_dc_voltage: {value: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit_symbol: \"V\"} — \"Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6 – 32 V\"\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_operating_range: {value: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- software_version: {value: \"03.00.03\"}\n- software_checksum: {value: \"B26AFE6B\"}\n- pressure_loss_class → config_level (axis: meter size).\n\nmodel_level:\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3: values per size: DN40 25, DN50 40, DN65 63, DN80 100, DN100 160, DN125 160, DN150 400, DN200 630, DN250 1000, DN300 1600, DN350 1600 (unit m³/h)\n- overload_flow_rate_q4: 31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000\n\nconfig_level:\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1, axis: \"meter size × accuracy class × power supply\", 36 values. Each: {condition: \"DN40, class 1, AC mains powered\", value: 0.05, unit_symbol: \"m³/h\"}.\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2: same 36.\n- ratio_q3_q1: same 36.\n\nHmm, this is very long. Is it worth it? The instruction says model/config extraction is the most important thing. And matrix_tables preserve the tables. Including config_level makes the data machine-usable without parsing matrix tables. I'll include. Actually... let me reconsider: maybe make the condition strings compact: \"DN40 / 1 / AC\". Define axis: \"meter_size / accuracy_class / power_supply\". Then condition: \"DN40 / 1 / AC mains\". And battery: \"DN40 / 1 & 2 / battery-renewable\". For \"1 & 2\" rows, class: \"1 & 2\". \n\nLet me write them out.\n\nAC mains (Table 2):\nq1:\n- \"DN40 / class 1 / AC mains\": 0.05\n- \"DN40 / class 2 / AC mains\": 0.025\n- \"DN50 / class 1 / AC mains\": 0.08\n- \"DN50 / class 2 / AC mains\": 0.04\n- \"DN65 / class 1 & 2 / AC mains\": 0.063\n- \"DN80 / class 1 & 2 / AC mains\": 0.1\n- \"DN100 / class 1 / AC mains\": 0.32\n- \"DN100 / class 2 / AC mains\": 0.16\n- \"DN125 / class 1 / AC mains\": 0.32\n- \"DN125 / class 2 / AC mains\": 0.16\n- \"DN150 / class 1 & 2 / AC mains\": 0.4\n- \"DN200 / class 1 & 2 / AC mains\": 0.63\n- \"DN250 / class 1 / AC mains\": 2\n- \"DN250 / class 2 / AC mains\": 1\n- \"DN300 / class 1 / AC mains\": 3.2\n- \"DN300 / class 2 / AC mains\": 1.6\n- \"DN350 / class 1 / AC mains\": 3.2\n- \"DN350 / class 2 / AC mains\": 1.6\n\nq2:\n- DN40/1: 0.08; DN40/2: 0.04\n- DN50/1: 0.128; DN50/2: 0.064\n- DN65: 0.1008\n- DN80: 0.16\n- DN100/1: 0.512; DN100/2: 0.256\n- DN125/1: 0.512; DN125/2: 0.256\n- DN150: 0.64\n- DN200: 1.008\n- DN250/1: 3.2; DN250/2: 1.6\n- DN300/1: 5.12; DN300/2: 2.56\n- DN350/1: 5.12; DN350/2: 2.56\n\nratio_q3_q1:\n- DN40/1: 500; DN40/2: 1000\n- DN50/1: 500; DN50/2: 1000\n- DN65: 1000\n- DN80: 1000\n- DN100/1: 500; DN100/2: 1000\n- DN125/1: 500; DN125/2: 1000\n- DN150: 1000\n- DN200: 1000\n- DN250/1: 500; DN250/2: 1000\n- DN300/1: 500; DN300/2: 1000\n- DN350/1: 500; DN350/2: 1000\n\nBattery/renewable (Table 3):\nq1:\n- DN40 / 1&2: 0.0625\n- DN50 / 1&2: 0.1\n- DN65/1: 0.39375; DN65/2: 0.1575\n- DN80/1: 0.625; DN80/2: 0.25\n- DN100/1: 1; DN100/2: 0.4\n- DN125/1: 1; DN125/2: 0.4\n- DN150 / 1&2: 1\n- DN200 / 1&2: 1.575\n- DN250/1: 6.25; DN250/2: 2.5\n- DN300/1: 10; DN300/2: 4\n- DN350/1: 10; DN350/2: 4\n\nq2:\n- DN40: 0.1\n- DN50: 0.16\n- DN65/1: 0.63; DN65/2: 0.252\n- DN80/1: 1; DN80/2: 0.4\n- DN100/1: 1.6; DN100/2: 0.64\n- DN125/1: 1.6; DN125/2: 0.64\n- DN150: 1.6\n- DN200: 2.52\n- DN250/1: 10; DN250/2: 4\n- DN300/1: 16; DN300/2: 6.4\n- DN350/1: 16; DN350/2: 6.4\n\nratio:\n- DN40: 400\n- DN50: 400\n- DN65/1: 160; DN65/2: 400\n- DN80/1: 160; DN80/2: 400\n- DN100/1: 160; DN100/2: 400\n- DN125/1: 160; DN125/2: 400\n- DN150: 400\n- DN200: 400\n- DN250/1: 160; DN250/2: 400\n- DN300/1: 160; DN300/2: 400\n- DN350/1: 160; DN350/2: 400\n\nOK. This is long but I'll write it out.\n\nWait — should I reconsider and simplify? The task says \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" This is part 1 of 2, so page 5 presumably has more (maybe orientation/installation info). I'll extract what's here.\n\nGiven the volume, I'll be systematic. The condition strings: I'll use format \"DN40, class 1, AC mains powered\" and \"DN40, class 1, battery/renewable powered\". Let me use \"AC mains\" and \"battery/renewable energy\".\n\nActually, hmm, another thought: maybe instead of separate config_level for q1/q2/ratio, I could set models to include power config: model_id like \"DN40 AC mains\" and \"DN40 battery\". Then q1/q2 still vary by class... no good. Config_level it is.\n\nmodel_id list: DN40, DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350 — 11 models.\n\nattributes in matrix rows: I'll name keys: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1_min (the note says Q1 is minimum value), q2_min, q3_max, q4_max, ratio_q3_q1. Hmm — the notes say \"Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; Values mentioned for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values\". So table values are minima for Q1/Q2 and maxima for Q3/Q4. I'll name columns q1, q2, q3, q4 and put the min/max semantics in footnotes/description. Keep column names simple: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. Actually maybe q1_min etc. encodes semantics nicely. But for model_level attributes I'll use \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\"? Hmm. Simpler: attribute \"q1\" with the footnote explaining. I'll use q1/q2/q3/q4 and rely on footnotes. Actually the schema example for R137 used \"qmax\", \"qt\", \"qmin\". I'll use \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\" — standard R49 naming.\n\nLet me also double check decimal conversions: \"0,05\"→0.05, \"0,08\"→0.08, \"31,25\"→31.25, \"0,128\"→0.128, \"0,1008\"→0.1008, \"78,75\"→78.75, \"0,063\"→0.063, \"0,16\"→0.16, \"0,32\"→0.32, \"0,512\"→0.512, \"0,256\"→0.256, \"0,4\"→0.4, \"0,64\"→0.64, \"0,63\"→0.63, \"1,008\"→1.008, \"3,2\"→3.2, \"1,6\"→1.6, \"2,56\"→2.56, \"5,12\"→5.12, \"1,6 MPa\"→1.6 MPa, \"+0,1\"→0.1, \"3,6\"→3.6, \"2,9\"→2.9, \"1,575\"→1.575, \"2,52\"→2.52, \"6,25\"→6.25, \"6,4\"→6.4, \"0,0625\"→0.0625, \"0,39375\"→0.39375, \"0,1575\"→0.1575, \"0,252\"→0.252, \"0,25\"→0.25.\n\nMAP: \"1,6 MPa (16 bar)\" → value 1.6, unit MPa. Could add footnote... I'll include as is. Maybe include both: value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\". Fine.\n\nWater temp: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\". value \"T50\", plus range.\n\nAmbient temp: \"+5 °C / +55 °C (for flow transmitter)\" → {min: 5, max: 55} °C; \"-25 °C / +55 °C (for flow sensor)\" → {min: -25, max: 55} °C.\n\nPower: \"AC mains: 85 –240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz\" → voltage {min:85, max:240} V; frequency {min:50, max:60} Hz; type AC. \"Renewable energy power (DC powered): 6 – 32 V\" → {min:6, max:32} V. \"Replaceable Battery: 3,6 V (operating range: 2,9 - 3,6 V)\" → 3.6 V, range {min:2.9, max:3.6} V.\n\nPressure loss class: config_level, axis \"meter size\":\n- {condition: \"DN40\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"} — keep as string with original \"Δp 40 (0,40 bar)\" → convert comma: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". \n- {condition: \"DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}\n- {condition: \"larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 65 (0.65 bar)\"}\n\nComponents: flow transmitter and flow sensor. Type designations: FEW4XX.R is maybe the sensor? Actually AquaMaster4: FET = flow transmitter? FEW = flow tube/water...? ABB AquaMaster: FEW4 = electromagnetic water flow sensor (wafer?), FET4 = transmitter (remote). The cert says \"Type: AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R and FET4*\". FEW4XX.R likely the flow sensor primary, FET4 the transmitter. But I shouldn't guess designations for components beyond what's stated. The footnote says second X can be 1,2,8,9 = \"(integral/remote version, optionally sensor only\". So FEW4XX is the sensor and FET4 the remote transmitter probably. But not explicitly stated; I'll leave type_designations empty for both components to avoid fabrication. Roles: \"Flow transmitter\", \"Flow sensor\".\n\nNow description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — certified_type.description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\". Category: \"Water meter\".\n\nmatrix_tables descriptions: name \"Reduce bore - AC mains powered\" (Table 2), and \"Reduce bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered\" (Table 3). Names: \"Table 2 – General characteristics of the family of instruments – Reduce bore – AC mains powered\". I'll use name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments - Reduce bore - AC mains powered\" and second similarly. Add footnotes lists.\n\nAlso the general \"Please note\" applies to flow rates — I'll include footnotes on both tables.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49-1\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [\"1\",\"2\"]. Hmm wait, canonical example: \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". The cert says requirements of R 49-1 (2013). Since id canonical examples drop edition, id \"R49-1\" retains part info. Good.\n\ntest_reports: [{id: \"NMi-2254799-01\", date: \"2020-08-14\", pages: 235, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}, {id: \"T11851-1\", role: \"documentation_file\"}]. The documentation folder — role documentation_file fits.\n\nfootnotes: [{\"marker\": \"*)\", \"text\": \"The type designation XX can be on the first X for 1 or 3 (standard or enhanced) and the second X can be 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only).\"}]\n\nShould I also add the flow-rate conditions as certificate-level footnotes? They're in matrix_tables footnotes. Fine.\n\nNow assemble the JSON. Be careful: no trailing commas, proper numbers.\n\nmodel_family: {family_name: \"AquaMaster4\", models: [...]}. Family name — the cert says \"the family of instruments\" referring to the AquaMaster4 types. I'll set family_name \"AquaMaster4\".\n\nLet me write the config_level arrays. Axis label: \"meter size / accuracy class / power supply\". Condition format: \"DN40, class 1, AC mains powered\". To keep entries compact I'll use short power labels: \"AC mains\" and \"battery/renewable energy\".\n\nFull q1 config values:\n\nAC mains:\n1. DN40 class 1: 0.05\n2. DN40 class 2: 0.025\n3. DN50 class 1: 0.08\n4. DN50 class 2: 0.04\n5. DN65 class 1&2: 0.063\n6. DN80 class 1&2: 0.1\n7. DN100 class 1: 0.32\n8. DN100 class 2: 0.16\n9. DN125 class 1: 0.32\n10. DN125 class 2: 0.16\n11. DN150 class 1&2: 0.4\n12. DN200 class 1&2: 0.63\n13. DN250 class 1: 2\n14. DN250 class 2: 1\n15. DN300 class 1: 3.2\n16. DN300 class 2: 1.6\n17. DN350 class 1: 3.2\n18. DN350 class 2: 1.6\n\nBattery:\n19. DN40 1&2: 0.0625\n20. DN50 1&2: 0.1\n21. DN65 1: 0.39375\n22. DN65 2: 0.1575\n23. DN80 1: 0.625\n24. DN80 2: 0.25\n25. DN100 1: 1\n26. DN100 2: 0.4\n27. DN125 1: 1\n28. DN125 2: 0.4\n29. DN150 1&2: 1\n30. DN200 1&2: 1.575\n31. DN250 1: 6.25\n32. DN250 2: 2.5\n33. DN300 1: 10\n34. DN300 2: 4\n35. DN350 1: 10\n36. DN350 2: 4\n\nSame for q2 and ratio.\n\nThis will be a long JSON. Acceptable.\n\nShould value include unit_symbol inside each value object? The structured value form for config_level: \"{condition, value, unit_symbol}\". So each value entry: {\"condition\": \"...\", \"value\": 0.05, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"}. I'll include unit_symbol on each (or omit where null). The schema shows {condition, value} with unit possibly. Example in rule 5: `{condition, value, unit_symbol}`. Include it.\n\nFor model_level: values: [{model, value}] — example didn't include unit per value; unit at attribute level: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}. Follow that.\n\nFor type_level: {label: {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}}. I'll include value and unit_symbol where applicable; include unit_id: null? The example shows unit_id optional-ish (\"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\"). I'll include unit_id: null in type_level entries for consistency? The strict shape says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers. Hmm — \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". To be safe, include unit_id: null and footnote_markers: [] where absent? That bloats. The example outputs in rules 4 omit them ({\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}). I'll include value + unit_symbol where non-null, and add footnote_markers only when referenced. I'll add unit_id: null for type_level entries to comply with the strict shape... Actually the strict rule says must be object with \"value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers\" shape. I'll include all four keys in type_level entries (footnote_markers omitted if empty? The shape includes it; I'll include as [] maybe). To reduce noise, I'll include \"unit_id\": null where there's a unit, and skip footnote_markers when empty. Hmm, mixed. Let me just include value, unit_symbol (when applicable), and footnote_markers only when present. That matches the examples given in the rules (the R137 example omits unit_id and footnote_markers). Good enough.\n\nNow the certificate date: \"14 August 2020\" → \"2020-08-14\".\n\nPage total: 5.\n\nLet me also decide type_designations: The type line: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R and FET4 *\". I'll list [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. The * is the footnote marker; the footnote explains XX wildcard. I'll keep \"AquaMaster4 FET4\" without *? The * marks the footnote, not part of designation. I'll include \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\" and \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"... Rule 1 says never include footnote markers in labels (characteristic labels). For type designations, cleaner to drop the *: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XX.R\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4\"]. And the footnote explains wildcards. Good.\n\nNow write final JSON.\n\nAlso: \"Reverse flow: The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — type_level reverse_flow value string.\n\n\"Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" → value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Keep as string. Or split: flow_profile_sensitivity_class \"U0 and D0\", straightness_requirements \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\". I'll keep one string.\n\nOrientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\".\n\nSoftware: version \"03.00.03\", checksum \"B26AFE6B\".\n\nMeasuring principle: \"Electromagnetic\".\n\nEnvironmental class handled in components.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"2254799\",\n    \"page_total\": 5,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2020-08-14\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"M. Boudewijns\",\n    \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n    \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n    \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"ABB Limited\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane\", \"GL10 3TA Stonehouse\", \"United Kingdom\"]\n    }\n  ],\n 